  "title": "Family",
  "description": "Learn words for family members and describe your household in Korean.",
  "guidebook": "### Talking About Family in Korean\n\nFamily terms in Korean vary depending on who is speaking:\n\n- **If you are male:** Call an older brother **형** (*hyeong*) and an older sister **누나** (*nuna*).\n- **If you are female:** Call an older brother **오빠** (*oppa*) and an older sister **언니** (*eonni*).\n\nFor a younger sibling, regardless of gender, use **동생** (*dongsaeng*). To specify, you can say **남동생** (*younger brother*) or **여동생** (*younger sister*).\n\n### Respect and Politeness\n\nKoreans often use honorific titles. For parents, **부모님** (*bumonim*) includes the polite suffix **-님** (*-nim*). When referring to your own family to others, use **우리** (*uri*, literally \"our\") instead of \"my\"—for example, **우리 가족** (*our family*).",
